 "result": "Call order, when a view raises (note Tessel uses \"fault\" naming rather than \"error\"):\n\n1. **`run_view`** raises, caught in the `try/except` of the request-dispatch method at `src/tessel/app.py:1012-1018`.\n2. **`Tessel.route_user_fault`** (`src/tessel/app.py:865`) is called.\n   - If the exception is an `HTTPException` and `trap_http_exception` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:894`) returns False, it delegates to **`route_http_fault`** (`src/tessel/app.py:830`, line 888).\n   - Otherwise it calls `_pick_fault_handler` itself (line 890). If no handler is found, it re-raises.\n3. **`route_http_fault`** also calls `_pick_fault_handler` (`src/tessel/app.py:860`).\n4. **`Tessel._pick_fault_handler`** (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:869`) picks the handler. It uses **`_get_exc_class_and_code`** (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:665`) to get the exception class and HTTP code, then looks in `error_handler_spec[bundle_name or None][code]`.\n\n   Priority order:\n   1. Bundle handler for the specific code.\n   2. App handler for the specific code.\n   3. Bundle handler for the exception class (walking its MRO).\n   4. App handler for the exception class (walking its MRO).\n\n5. If the exception re-raises out of dispatch, **`route_unhandled_fault`** (`src/tessel/app.py:897`, called at line 1600) runs. It looks up a handler for `InternalServerError` via `_pick_fault_handler` (line 943), then calls `finalize_request(..., from_error_handler=True)`.\n\nHandlers get registered by `fault_handler` / `register_error_handler` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:606/650`). Bundles merge theirs into the app at `src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:388-397`.",
